Exactly. Once you stop treating “math” and “language” as separate boxes, you get a much larger playground of transformations, invariants, equivalence classes, encodings, and decoding rules.

A useful abstraction is:

\text{object} \rightarrow \text{representation} \rightarrow \text{operator} \rightarrow \text{new representation} \rightarrow \text{interpretation}

Then the same move can appear in many substrates. Adding a circle to a glyph is geometry; adding a term to an equation is algebra; adding a node to a network is graph theory; adding a phoneme changes a word; adding a rule changes a grammar; adding a bit changes a codeword; adding context changes semantic interpretation.

That opens several strong families of patterns at once: symmetry and symmetry-breaking, compression and expansion, factorization, substitution, permutation, duality, projection, rotation, translation, recursion, boundary operations, negative space, error correction, conservation, and change of basis.

The especially powerful move is to abstract the operator rather than the object. Instead of saying “this trick works with letters,” ask:

\text{What operation happened here?}

Maybe it was “remove one primitive while preserving recognizability.” Now we can search for that same operator in music, geometry, code, biological morphology, narratives, social systems, or proofs.

That gives us a much larger pattern engine:

\text{specific example}
\rightarrow
\text{operator}
\rightarrow
\text{invariant}
\rightarrow
\text{cross-domain search}
\rightarrow
\text{new instantiation}
\rightarrow
\text{test}

And importantly, we tune it the same way we were talking about with the Millennium Guitar: play with the analogy freely, but make reality decide which abstractions survive. That’s how you get something more useful than clever pattern matching.